sdl2_drastic: adopt SDL KMSDRM EGL context on rk3566 + threaded video

Two-part patch for rk3568/rk3566 (guarded by ADVDRASTIC_SDL_GL, other
targets unchanged):

1. Adopt SDL's KMSDRM GL context on video_handler thread.

trngaje's video_handler thread ran its own DRM + GBM + EGL in parallel
with SDL's KMSDRM backend. On the rockchip mali-blob-gbm variant this
didn't work (gbm_bo_get_handle isn't a valid DRM GEM handle so
drmModeAddFB fails EINVAL) and even if it did, we'd fight SDL for DRM
master.

Now: main releases its GL context via SDL_GL_MakeCurrent(win, NULL) on
first render (release_main_gl_context_once, called from all three main-
thread signaller sites - sdl_update_screens, sdl_update_screen_menu,
display_custom_setting). We save (win, ctx) first because SDL nulls
current_glctx on release. Video_handler lazy-inits egl_init on first
GFX_Flip/GFX_Copy - by then the release has happened, so
SDL_GL_MakeCurrent(saved_win, saved_ctx) migrates the context to this
thread cleanly. GFX_Flip uses SDL_GL_SwapWindow (not raw eglSwapBuffers)
so the full KMSDRM lock_front / AddFB / PageFlip pipeline runs, which
is what actually promotes the GBM front buffer to the scanout plane.

egl_swapinterval is poked to 0 directly through _this->egl_data
(bypassing knulli's SetSwapInterval clamp) so async pageflip is at
least requested - kernel 4.19 doesn't support it, so no observed win,
but harmless.

egl_terminate releases the context from this thread; doesn't destroy
the SDL-owned handles.

2. Threaded-video decoupling.

Even with the context migration working, SDL_GL_SwapWindow on this
stack takes ~30ms per frame (one full vsync interval spent in
WaitPageflip because the legacy drmModePageFlip API only allows one
pending flip). Old sdl_update_screens did cond_signal + cond_wait for
response, blocking main for that entire ~30ms every frame. NDS
emulator capped at ~18fps and audio stuttered.

Now: main snapshots drastic's NDS screen pixels into one of 2 ring
slots (snap_slot[]), atomically publishes it as snap_consumer_idx
under snap_mutex, signals video_handler, and returns without waiting.
Video_handler grabs the latest slot into snap_current_idx and calls
process_screen (which reads pixels from snap_slot[current_idx]
instead of the raw VAR_SDL_SCREEN* pointers, avoiding races with
drastic's ongoing emulation). Video_handler releases the outer lock
during render+flip so main isn't blocked on mutex acquisition.

Standard emulator threaded-video pattern (as in RetroArch, PCSX2,
PPSSPP): emulation rate and render rate are independent, intermediate
frames get dropped.

Measured: 2D scenes now hit 60fps (up from 18). 3D scenes limited by
NDS 3D emulation CPU cost, not by us.

Signed-off-by: James Pharaoh <james@pharaoh.uk>
